## The argument

### The tenant has already told you what Terminal F is

In July 2026 American Airlines rolled out its "next-generation premium experience at DFW." The announcement named a 37,000-square-foot Admirals Club in Terminal C, adjacent to the Skylink station — described as the airline's largest club anywhere in its network. It named a Flagship Check-In facility in Terminal D near the D30 checkpoint, serving Flagship First, Flagship Business, and eligible AAdvantage elites through a separate entrance and dedicated processing. It named, for Terminal F, a Provisions by Admirals Club grab-and-go concept: a scaled-down offering for transit passengers with short layovers, not a Flagship Lounge and not a full Admirals Club.[^4]

This is the party with the most information about its own 2043 network telling DFW where the premium program lives in the physical footprint. It is also the airline's revealed preference on which of DFW's five terminals it treats as the premium anchor, and it is not the terminal DFW is now building.

The run prompt names American's premium pivot as the reason to design Terminal F to absorb it. That premise is right about the pivot and wrong about the terminal. The premium pivot is real — Delta's Q4 2025 premium ticket revenue of $5.70B crossed main cabin at $5.62B for the first time in Delta's history, full-year 2025 premium reached $22.1B against $23.4B main cabin, and American's own targets call for narrowbody premium share to move from ~25% to ~40% of capacity and international lie-flat count to grow more than 50% by end of decade.[^6] The 787-9P is a 244-seat aircraft carrying 51 Flagship Suites, versus 285 seats and 30 Flagship on the previous configuration.[^17] That is a structural yield play with physical implications for widebody gate mix, lounge seat pitch, and premium check-in envelope.

But American is executing that pivot at DFW through Terminal C and Terminal D. Terminal F is being scoped, on American's own announcement, as a connecting building with a grab-and-go. The Skylink station in Terminal F, included in Phase 1 and connecting to the Terminal C Admirals Club adjacent to its own Skylink stop, is beginning to look less like a premium terminal and more like a piece of a distribution spine — an arrivals-facing extension of a premium anchor that lives one Skylink station away.

Two things follow. First, the design brief is not "premium terminal." It is generic hub geometry, with fit-out latitude that American can exercise as its network math evolves. Second, DFW should treat American's Provisions designation as a live signal about 2026 capital allocation, not a settled decision about 2043 usage. There is a real difference between honoring the signal — do not build a Flagship Lounge the tenant did not ask for — and refusing to preserve the physical envelope that would let the tenant change its mind in 2032 without a nine-figure mid-lease retrofit. The reversibility register is what makes that distinction executable.

### The bank is getting denser, not flatter

American is doing a second thing at DFW that cuts against a premium reading of the Terminal F floor plate. In December 2025 the airline announced a restructure from 9 to 13 daily connecting banks starting April 2026 — "doubling down on DFW... further strengthens its Flagship hub" in the airline's own framing.[^18] The 9 a.m. peak departures figure drops from ~110 to ~52; 40-plus flights move into hours (11 a.m., 1 p.m., 3 p.m., 5 p.m., 9 p.m.) that used to be trough; scheduled block times are being lengthened by an average of 6 minutes across 145 markets to buy connection reliability.[^19]

The most-corroborated read is that this is a doubling-down on connecting complexity, not a shift toward originating premium. Bank contiguity replaces peak-hour surge; gate concurrency at Terminal F is set by how tightly the 13 banks butt up against each other. That is the opposite of the physical implication a premium-shift thesis would draw, which is a lower-density, higher-dwell, more originator-friendly floor.

Two caveats. The COO reads the same announcement as evidence of a *flatter*, mid-day-loaded operation, which if right would soften the density case. And American's own reported 50% reduction in missed connections is two weeks post-launch, self-reported.[^20] The operational data are more sober: DFW's Q2 2026 departure rate is up only ~1% year over year and remains second-worst among busy US airports, and the +6-minute block-time additions mechanically inflate scheduled on-time performance.[^21] The rebank's benefits are asserted; not proven. Terminal F should be sized to the unproven-benefit case rather than to the press release.

The connecting-share number is contested and matters. DFW airport-level reporting places connecting share at about 60%; the run prompt asserts a carrier-level figure of about two-thirds. Neither has a primary AA document.[^22] Between 55% and 70% connecting, the peak-day originating count swings by roughly 50% — 30,000 vs. 45,000 O&D passenger-trips at American's ~100,000 peak-day.[^23] Curbside, check-in hall depth, and TSA lane count are structural and expensive to move after pour; lounge, gate hold, and concessions are fit-out. The design implication is not which figure to believe; it is which of the four structural elements has to be sized to hold either.

### History is not neutral about the shape of this building

The industry has run this play — a purpose-built or purpose-adapted hub terminal on a long single-carrier commitment — several times in the deregulation era. Take the post-1978 US set at non-trivial hub scale. Four cases went badly when the carrier repositioned. One case aged well through a merger. One is instructive but inconclusive because the carrier is still there. That inclusion criterion is stated so the sample is auditable rather than curated; the record is not a base rate, it is a set of cases doing the work of cases.[^5]

Pittsburgh's Midfield Terminal opened October 1, 1992 at roughly $1 billion, with an X-shaped airside optimized for the US Airways connecting bank. US Airways dehubbed in 2004 after a fee dispute the airport could not resolve. Cost per enplaned passenger rose to nearly $15 by 2011, roughly twice what airlines had paid in 2002.[^24] Pittsburgh spent the next fifteen years running a hub airport after the hub had left; the stranded midfield debt was not extinguished until roughly 2019, and a new $1.75B landside terminal opened in 2025 essentially undoing the 1992 geometry.[^25]

Cincinnati went from 22.7 million passengers and 600-plus daily flights in 2005 to under 6 million and under 180 daily by 2013 as Delta wound down its second-largest hub.[^26] St. Louis fell from more than 500 daily American/TWA flights in July 2001 to 207 by 2003, driven by the ORD slot restriction lifting in 2002; American closed Concourses B and D and retreated to Concourse C.[^27] Cleveland's Concourse D — the newer hub concourse — went dark in May 2014 after United's dehub, with destinations falling from 61 to 20 and daily departures from about 200 to 72.[^28]

The affirmative case in the file is Detroit McNamara. It opened in 2002, designed by SmithGroup for Northwest Airlines as a linear midfield with satellite concourses.[^7] Northwest merged into Delta in 2008; Delta kept and still operates the terminal as its DTW hub building. Two honest caveats. First, the design-intent record for McNamara — including cost and post-merger capital history — is drawn from trade-press and enthusiast archives, not from Wayne County Airport Authority primaries; treat the specific dollar figures as directional and the "no material retrofit" claim as the design read available to us rather than an audited absence. Second, the case is a survivable-merger case, not a dehub-survival case: the successor kept the hub. The lesson McNamara supports is narrower than the lesson Pittsburgh warns against — but it is the right lesson for a Terminal F design brief. A hub form that is not carrier-specific in its structural moves (linear midfield, satellite concourses, generic connecting-bank geometry, redundant vertical circulation at the transit node) can absorb a change of tenant intent without material retrofit. It does not decide whether the hub survives; it decides whether the building does.

Two honest cautions about the historical file. The dehub cases are survivorship-biased in favor of drama: DFW, ATL, DEN, ORD, IAH, MSP, DTW, SLC, SEA, PHX, and CLT have all held their hub carriers through the entire consolidation wave, and the base rate for a super-hub of DFW's scale being dehubbed inside a 20-year forward window is low. And Pittsburgh's 80% single-carrier concentration on a much smaller regional O&D floor is not a like-for-like analogue for DFW's 82.6% American share on a two-city O&D economy of roughly eight million people plus regional output estimated by regional press at up to $71.1B, magnitude only.[^29] The historical warning applies most sharply to the *shape of the building*, not to the *survival of the hub*. That is the argument's point. Terminal F's shape should be encoded generically enough to survive a strategy shift that history says will arrive — whether or not the carrier does.

### The operative deadline is release-for-fabrication, per module

The run prompt phrases the design-lock horizon as "before the concrete cures." That framing is technically wrong for a modular build, and it is time to say so plainly. The relevant deadline is closer to "before the module is skinned." For the first six Phase 1 modules — the ones placed during the two-week overnight window in August 2026 — it has already passed.[^1]

Modular construction is not a suspension of the physics of retrofit. Managing tolerance stack-up between two large modules fabricated on separate shifts, transported by SPMT, and placed to three-quarter-inch survey is genuinely difficult; when it goes wrong it does not present as a headline, it presents as leaking curtain wall at the module seam, misaligned baggage take-away belts, or life-safety commissioning findings — the exact failure modes that put Berlin Brandenburg nine years and roughly 2.3× budget over.[^30] The 30% savings figure DFW cites versus conventional construction is credible for on-site duration and for reduction of airside impact windows.[^8] What the number is not is a reduction in the design-lock horizon. Modular pushes design-freeze earlier, because once a module is being cut, welded, and MEP-fit in the factory, the design has to be locked well ahead of the stick-built equivalent.

A category-based reversibility map, priced honestly, does more useful work than a generic "flexibility" claim.

**Category A — structurally irreversible once modules are set and utilities tied in.** Column grid and floor-to-floor heights. Sterile-corridor geometry into the FIS. Primary baggage tunnel alignment and depth. The apron column-and-jetway grid that determines whether a stand is Code C, Code E, or MARS. Hydrant fuel loop capacity and hydrant pit locations. Primary electrical service and standby generator sizing. 400 Hz and PCA capacity at the stand. The Skylink node, including platform-edge count and vertical circulation redundancy. These are pour-locked; some are release-for-fabrication-locked.

**Category B — reversible only at high schedule and airline-consent cost.** Lounge shell subdivisions inside preserved floor plate. Flagship arrival vestibule and dedicated elevator core. CBP FIS booth counts within a preserved hall. TSA checkpoint lane count within preserved queue geometry. BHS in-line EDS matrix within a preserved conveyor spine. These are exactly the decisions where preserving Category-A envelope now — risers, structural allowance, curtainwall breaks, MEP shafts — buys Category-C reversibility later.

**Category C — cheap to defer, cheap to change later.** Interior finishes and wayfinding. Retail and concession fit-out. Furniture and lounge zoning. Low-voltage overlays: biometrics, digital signage, sensor networks — provided that conduit, riser space, IDF closets, and PoE++ budget were sized generously in Category A.

The strongest, cheapest engineering move DFW can make now is to buy Category-C flexibility by paying Category-A prices in a small, named set of places. Oversize the risers. Oversize the IDF closets. Oversize the BHS spine. Preserve MARS provisions at a defined minority of stands. That is what "flexible" means when it is priced honestly.

### The 6:15 morning

Every claim above is easier to see if you walk the building on a specific morning. The composite below is built from ledger facts; it is a composite because the airport-side design specifics of the Terminal F Skylink station are not in the public record.[^12]

05:55. Skylink is still running its overnight single-loop pattern — one direction only, roughly 15-minute end-to-end transit, exactly as it does every night from 22:00 to 06:00.[^12] The first of American's 13 daily banks is already inbound. Under the restructured schedule the airline expects roughly 52 departures in the 9 a.m. peak — bank contiguity replacing the old surge — which implies a peak-bank population on the order of 5,500 passengers at ~107 passengers per departure per the run's `peak_departure_load_factor`, before any stranded fraction from the previous bank.[^31] Now hold that geometry against the last week of January 2023: winter storm ground stop, four-hour post-stop average delays, more than 1,100 cancellations on the peak day and more than 600 the day after.[^32] Ask which parts of Terminal F's geometry decide whether that morning propagates or dampens.

Four chokepoints do the work. The Skylink node inside Terminal F — platform edges, vertical circulation redundancy, concourse-side queue depth on a morning when the second loop fails to return at 06:00.[^12] Gate concurrency under bank contiguity — how many bank-contiguous gates can turn simultaneously without spillover into taxilane object-free areas defined by FAA AC 150/5300-13B.[^33] MARS stands as the pressure valve that lets one position work either a widebody international arrival or two narrowbody bank turns.[^11] The FIS — CBP's ~100 pax/hr per double booth and 50–75 ft primary queue depth, against a scheduled single-quarter signal in which American is targeting a Q3 2026 DFW capacity expansion of about 6% year over year alongside a roughly 13% LHR reduction, and has added six new summer 2026 international routes (Budapest, Prague, Athens, Milan, Zurich, and expanded Buenos Aires).[^10] [^34]

Every one is a Category-A decision locked at release-for-fabrication. If the Skylink station opens without a reserved second platform edge, redundant vertical circulation, and concourse-side queuing sized for an unscheduled single-loop morning, the failure mode is not delayed connections. The failure mode is the connecting bank collapsing — which is the operational metric the airport and the airline both need to defend. If the FIS is sized to a 2025 international peak rather than to a properly commissioned 2043 forecast, the airport buys the geometry back with CBP staffing overtime for thirty years.

Premium at Terminal F, on this morning, is what survives the morning: reliable connections, a working Skylink node, an FIS that does not queue back into the sterile corridor. The Flagship-convertible shell rides along as a priced option, not as the headline.

### The register is the artifact the board should sign

The Council's independent research swarm on this run converged on a written reversibility register as the highest-value deliverable — a convergence worth naming and worth discounting. The run prompt itself asked the Council for a "reversibility map," so the vote is not clean corroboration; treat it as agreement on a framing the prompt supplied. What is not prompt-seeded is the underlying reversibility economics. The single case in the file where the building absorbed a change of tenant intent without material retrofit — Detroit McNamara — did so because it encoded generic hub geometry. The four cases where the building did not absorb the change spent decades and, in Pittsburgh's case, a $1.75B replacement terminal correcting for structural moves that were carrier-specific in fixed structure. The argument for the register does not rest on the vote; it rests on the arithmetic.

The register, as an instrument, does three things. It converts an unbounded design debate into a bounded, priced governance instrument. It carries every Category-A decision through the fabrication schedule with a last-responsible-moment date, a cost order of magnitude, an accountable executive owner, and — critically — an explicit test of whether American's consent is required under exclusive-use provisions. And it imposes an operating rule: for any option priced above a defined threshold, a signed counterparty commitment before the option is bought. That last provision is the pricing discipline that keeps the register honest. It absorbs the strongest single objection in the run's evidence base — that preserved options accumulate into a shopping list no future budget funds — as the register's operating rule, not as an argument against it. The register is not a wish list. It is a priced cull.

The DFW Board is 12 members, 11 voting, with 7 seats appointed by Dallas and 4 by Fort Worth and a rotating non-voting neighbor seat.[^35] It meets monthly. A CEO fifteen months into the job, appointed by unanimous board vote out of an operations background, is presiding over a $11.3B CIP through FY30 of which Terminals F, C, and A renewal are the three largest components — programs competing for the same craft labor, fabrication yards, crane and SPMT windows, and airside impact windows.[^36] The register is the mechanism through which McLaughlin runs the design calendar as a standing monthly item, and through which the board can audit — and the rating agencies can cite — documented governance discipline over the CIP's largest single scope.

Four options belong on the register on day one, priced against the arithmetic:

- **A MARS-capable minority of stands** — the pour-locked flex that lets a Terminal F position work either a widebody premium routing or two narrowbody bank turns. Cheap at fabrication (deeper apron, dual PLBs, dual 400 Hz and PCA, higher-capacity hydrant fuel); unavailable after.[^11]
- **A Flagship-convertible lounge shell** in the 19,000–40,000 sf range — structural allowance, MEP risers, curtainwall breaks, back-of-house elevator core. Preserving the shell is a Category-A cost in single-digit percent of the base program; *building* the lounge at grade would displace roughly 1.58 to 4.00 gate positions on industry-rule-of-thumb floorplates of 10,000–12,000 sf per narrowbody gate, at $129M–$150M per gate — $204M at the low case, $600M at the high case, before fit-out.[^37] The load-bearing assumption is at-grade siting; a mezzanine placement, as at Delta One JFK T4, displaces roughly zero gates and collapses this arithmetic. The shell is the option. The lounge — mezzanine or at-grade — is a decision American takes later, on American's balance sheet, on the industry pattern that put Delta's ~40,000-sf Delta One JFK T4 lounge on Delta's side of the line.[^38]
- **An FIS envelope sized to a properly commissioned 2043 international peak-hour forecast** — with the forecast itself commissioned as a deliverable before the FIS module release-for-fabrication, and with the design sized to it. Do not size to a 2025 peak. Do not size to a single-quarter scheduled signal like the current LHR/DFW shift. Do commission the forecast. CBP's ~100 pax/hr per double booth benchmark, 50–75 ft primary queue depth, sterile-corridor width to hold the queue without contamination, escalator core count sized for the widebody arrival wave — all fall out of the forecast once it exists.[^10] Dallas Resolution 25-1461's CBP FIS reimbursement action is the earliest public procedural signal that international throughput is a live design conversation.[^39]
- **A Skylink node with a reserved second platform edge, redundant vertical circulation, and concourse-side queueing sized for a morning when the second loop fails to return at 06:00**. Category-A. If not preserved in Phase 1, the retrofit is punitive and interferes with an operating Skylink.

Two more options belong on the register but need a settled contractual answer first. CUPPS-capable gate hardware — an open-standard shell allowing shared physical gate positions with swappable airline software[^40] — is IROPS insurance the operator wants; whether it can be built without triggering an MII objection under the exclusive-use envelope is a legal-architecture question that belongs at the U&L amendment table. A dedicated gate-camera and Assaia-ready mounting envelope with sized PoE++ budget hedges turn-analytics deployment without betting on the vendor ROI figures (Assaia's YYZ 44% taxi-in reduction and Alaska/SEA 12% turn reduction are vendor-published; treat as directional).[^41] Both are register candidates. Neither is a register commitment until the contract question is answered.

The single most important open question on the register is not a design number. It is the contract number: whether the 2025 U&L extension obligates American to any Flagship-tier lounge presence at Terminal F before 2043. If yes, the shell is a design decision and belongs on the fabrication calendar. If no, it is a real-options decision and belongs at the ratemaking table. The evidence base does not answer the question, and the answer determines whether the reversibility discussion is design or contract.[^42]

