## Why the counter-case is insufficient — and where it isn't

The counter-case is right about three things and the recommendation concedes them.

American has publicly designated Provisions at Terminal F and that is a live signal. The industry finance norm puts premium fit-out on the carrier's balance sheet and DFW should not preload the *lounge*. Every preserved option carries a premium; option pricing discipline has to be the register's operating rule, not an argument against it. Those concessions are why the register requires a signed American cost-recovery memorandum for any option priced above a defined threshold, and why it does not recommend that DFW *build* a Flagship Lounge — only that DFW preserve the physical envelope inside which one could later be built, on American's decision, on American's balance sheet, at fit-out cost rather than at retrofit cost.

The counter-case is insufficient on two counts.

First, the historical record on what happens to a purpose-built single-carrier hub terminal when the carrier's strategy changes. The case in the file where the building absorbed the change without material retrofit did so by encoding a hub *form* — MARS-capable minority stands, generic connecting-bank geometry, an FIS sized to the international peak of its era, redundant vertical circulation at the transit node — that was cheap at pour and expensive to add later. None of those provisions is a premium bet on American's 2035 strategy. They are geometry bets on the class of hub Terminal F is, not on which airline flies through it. The counter-case's discipline about carrier-specific premium spend is right; extending that discipline to refuse generic hub geometry — MARS, FIS, Skylink node redundancy — is the historical error the DTW case identifies. Buying the shape of the building is not the same as buying the tenant's marketing.

Second, the arithmetic asymmetry between preserving an option now and retrofitting it later. Concourse D at Dulles carried a design decision — no AeroTrain station — that was cheap at pour and generational to reverse; the mobile-lounge dependency that decision preserved has been in continuous operation since 1962 and the AeroTrain extension and underground tunnel to correct the omission is now budgeted at ~$3.75B inside a $22.5B MWAA renovation program that is inclusive of financing and inflation and runs 2028–2033.[^16] Denver's baggage program spent more than $100M on terminal-design changes to fit a system United abandoned in 2005.[^47] LaGuardia Terminal C — the closest single-airline governance analogue in the record — delivered its four-concourse, $4B program roughly two years ahead of schedule; the analogue is instructive precisely because ~$3.5B of that $4B was Delta's capital and ~$500M was Port Authority.[^48] Read as a supporting precedent for airport-funded Terminal F, LGA does not carry the analogy: the airline had the capital at risk and the governance was aligned with that risk. Read as a discipline test — was single-tenant delivery on schedule and on budget when the airline held the pen — LGA is affirmative. Heathrow T5 was delivered on time within £4.3B, then lost 42,000 bags on opening when operational readiness proved undersized.[^49] The record, held together: single-airline governance delivers when the party accountable holds the capital, and the retrofit premium on decisions structurally omitted at pour is generational. The register's Category-A options — low-single-digit percent of a $4B base program — are cheaper than the retrofit they hedge against by orders of magnitude, even discounted for the probability the option is never triggered.

The counter-case's narrow position — "buy two or three specific options on named gate positions with signed AA cost-recovery, refuse everything else" — is not the register's opponent. It is the register's operating rule at the *lounge* item. It is not the operating rule at the *geometry* items, because MARS provisions, FIS envelope, and Skylink node redundancy are not premium bets. They are generic hub bets, and the tenant does not need to sign a memorandum for the airport to buy the shape of its own building. Right in the narrow; insufficient in the general. The register keeps both true.

### What 2035 has to look like for the recommendation to be wrong

Three falsification conditions, stated plainly.

The recommendation is wrong if by 2035 American has funded a Flagship-tier lounge at Terminal F on its own balance sheet ahead of what the register anticipated — DFW's preserved shell was unnecessarily conservative. Cost of being wrong: a few years of lease revenue on the reserved footprint. Modest.

The recommendation is wrong if connecting share at DFW has fallen materially below the 55–70% band the sizing rests on — plausible driver: A321XLR-scale ultra-long-narrowbody flying eroding the connect premium — in which case connecting-hall depth, gate-hold sizing, and Skylink node capacity are overbuilt.[^50] Cost of being wrong: lower gate-utilization density on the connecting side, offset by a higher one on the O&D side, with a check-in hall that has to be re-scoped.

The recommendation is wrong if CPE has breached the $20–22 behavioral threshold the airline commercial reading identifies — plausible given DWU's FY26 projection at $16.99, projected total debt of $12.4B by FY29, and Terminal F debt not yet fully layered — and American is contesting every optional dollar.[^51] Cost of being wrong: the register becomes a rate-negotiation instrument rather than a design instrument.

The third is the largest failure mode and has an implication the register makes visible: if CPE breaches the threshold, the case for buying options at pour prices rather than retrofit prices *strengthens*, not weakens — mid-lease debt to fund a retrofit inside a hostile CPE environment is the exact scenario the airport most wants to avoid.

