## Implications for the operator

The decisions that follow.

**Adopt the reversibility register as a standing capital-governance artifact.** Board action, one board cycle. Owner: CEO's office, with the Terminal F program executive as delegate. Deliverable: a module-by-module register naming every Category-A decision with last-responsible-moment date, cost order of magnitude, accountable owner, and whether American's consent is required. Cadence: reviewed at each board meeting, updated on the fabrication schedule. Stop condition: any Category-A decision whose last-responsible-moment date arrives without a board-approved disposition.

**Price and commit four Category-A geometry options in the next fabrication release tranche.** MARS-capable minority of stands at a defined percentage of Phase 2 positions (deeper apron, dual PLBs, dual 400 Hz and PCA, higher-capacity hydrant fuel). Flagship-convertible lounge *shell* — structural allowance, MEP risers, curtainwall breaks, back-of-house elevator core, sized to the mezzanine option first and to at-grade as an alternate; *not* the lounge. FIS envelope sized to a commissioned 2043 international peak-hour forecast, against CBP's ~100 pax/hr per double booth and 50–75 ft queue depth. Skylink node with reserved second platform edge, redundant vertical circulation sized for full station evacuation, and concourse-side queueing depth sized for a morning the second loop fails to return at 06:00. Federal dependencies: FAA AC 150/5300-13B for MARS geometry; CBP consultation and Reimbursable Services Program treatment for FIS scope; TSA concurrence on checkpoint sizing and any Flagship-priority routing.

**Commission the 2043 international peak-hour forecast now.** Owner: CEO's office with airline properties and planning. Timing: before the FIS module release-for-fabrication. The register cannot size the FIS honestly against a target that does not exist in the evidence base; commissioning the target is itself a Category-A decision.

**Open the U&L side-letter conversation on the two contract questions the register cannot answer without it** — the Flagship-tier lounge obligation before 2043 and the CUPPS-capable gate hardware treatment under the exclusive-use envelope. Owner: CEO's office with legal and airline properties. Timing: before the Phase 2 fabrication release tranche that would preload the shell.

**Fund the operational-readiness discipline the historical record has been naming since ACRP Synthesis 20.**[^52] Seat operations at module-level factory design reviews with veto authority. Witness testing at the factory. Contract each module seam as a mapped "operator interface plane" so MEP, low-voltage, and BHS branches can be re-terminated in 2038 without breaching structure. Deliverable: a written specification before the next module design lock, deposited in DFW's CMMS at commissioning.

**Fund the second bill.** Terminal F capitalized at $4B carries a 30-year O&M profile — baggage-handling machinery on a 15-year replacement cycle at roughly 10% of purchase cost per year in EDS maintenance, HVAC and BMS at 15–20 years, apron pavement first meaningful rehab in the 10–15 year window under AC 150/5380-7B, PBBs on 20–25-year cycles with mid-life overhauls.[^53] Underfunding by 15% generates a nine-figure deferred-maintenance liability inside 20 years. That number belongs in the capital plan the register lives inside, not in the discovery of a 2038 board.

**Publish the register externally at the annual credit review.** It is the single artifact that turns Terminal F's design case from ambition into governance discipline the rating agencies can cite. Rating agencies cite documented governance discipline in their commentary on airport credits; that is the kind of instrument the register is. Do not overstate the payoff — the basis-point differential attributable specifically to publishing a register is not measurable in the evidence base — but publish it because it is auditable, not because it is monetizable.

---

## Close

The building is not the argument. The register is.

Terminal F opens in phases from 2027 through full opening in 2030, and operates against a lease that expires in 2043 — thirteen to sixteen years of runway depending on where you start the clock. Across that span the carrier's strategy will change more than once, the connecting-bank geometry is already changing, the fleet mix is already changing, and the federal-partner staffing model changes annually. The single question the design has to answer is not *what does American want in 2026*. It is: *what shape can the building hold, at low marginal cost bought at fabrication, that survives every plausible turn American takes between now and 2043?*

The tenant's answer is on the record: Terminal F is not the premium anchor. Believe it. Build the connecting building the tenant is asking for. And then, on the airport's own side of the line, buy the small list of geometry options — MARS, FIS envelope, Skylink node, convertible shell — that let the tenant change its mind on the tenant's balance sheet in 2032 without a mid-lease retrofit on the order of the shell displacement itself (call it $200M, that being the low-case foregone gate value the register makes visible) in 2035.

The register is where that discipline gets signed. The board should sign it, on the fabrication calendar's clock, before the next module is skinned.

